1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:02,610
There's a quote that's been going around the internet for years:

2
00:00:02,630 --> 00:00:05,391
compound interest is the eighth wonder of the world.

3
00:00:05,411 --> 00:00:09,037
People say Einstein said it, but he almost certainly never did.

4
00:00:09,057 --> 00:00:12,464
One thing is true, though: compound interest can change your future.

5
00:00:12,484 --> 00:00:15,597
Today I'll show you how it works, how to really save money, and

6
00:00:15,617 --> 00:00:18,867
why a one hundred percent safe investment doesn't exist.

7
00:00:18,887 --> 00:00:19,807
Get comfortable.

8
00:00:19,827 --> 00:00:21,077
Let's start with the basics.

9
00:00:21,097 --> 00:00:24,157
You put away one thousand dollars, and it earns four percent a

10
00:00:24,177 --> 00:00:24,690
year.

11
00:00:24,710 --> 00:00:27,397
After one year, you have forty dollars in interest.

12
00:00:27,417 --> 00:00:28,777
Nothing magical so far.

13
00:00:28,797 --> 00:00:32,477
But the next year, that four percent is calculated on one thousand

14
00:00:32,497 --> 00:00:34,493
and forty, not on one thousand.

15
00:00:34,513 --> 00:00:36,264
Your interest starts earning interest.

16
00:00:36,284 --> 00:00:39,405
It's like a snowball rolling downhill: the bigger it gets, the

17
00:00:39,425 --> 00:00:40,657
more snow it picks up.

18
00:00:40,677 --> 00:00:44,363
And here's a trick to do the math in your head: the rule of seventy-two.

19
00:00:44,383 --> 00:00:47,325
Divide seventy-two by the interest rate, and you get roughly how

20
00:00:47,345 --> 00:00:49,224
many years it takes to double your money.

21
00:00:49,244 --> 00:00:51,576
At four percent, about eighteen years.

22
00:00:51,596 --> 00:00:52,806
Now look at this example.

23
00:00:52,826 --> 00:00:55,913
Anna starts at twenty-five and puts away one hundred dollars a

24
00:00:55,933 --> 00:00:57,734
month, until she's sixty-five.

25
00:00:57,754 --> 00:01:00,939
Mark does exactly the same, but he starts at thirty-five.

26
00:01:00,959 --> 00:01:04,379
At four percent a year, before taxes and fees, Anna ends up with

27
00:01:04,399 --> 00:01:07,270
about ONE HUNDRED EIGHTEEN THOUSAND dollars.

28
00:01:07,290 --> 00:01:09,406
Mark, with about sixty-nine thousand.

29
00:01:09,426 --> 00:01:12,466
Ten years late, and almost fifty thousand dollars less.

30
00:01:12,486 --> 00:01:14,486
The secret isn't how much you put in.

31
00:01:14,506 --> 00:01:15,746
It's WHEN you start.

32
00:01:15,766 --> 00:01:18,630
And careful: leaving money idle has a cost too.

33
00:01:18,650 --> 00:01:21,974
With two percent inflation, ten thousand dollars under the mattress

34
00:01:21,994 --> 00:01:25,286
will, after twenty years, buy what six thousand seven hundred

35
00:01:25,306 --> 00:01:26,156
buys today.

36
00:01:26,176 --> 00:01:29,766
And in twenty twenty-two, inflation in the US, the UK and the

37
00:01:29,786 --> 00:01:32,336
eurozone reached eight percent or more.

38
00:01:33,373 --> 00:01:34,747
So, how do you start?

39
00:01:34,767 --> 00:01:36,333
First: an emergency fund.

40
00:01:36,353 --> 00:01:39,732
Three to six months of expenses, somewhere safe and always available,

41
00:01:39,752 --> 00:01:40,948
like a savings account.

42
00:01:40,968 --> 00:01:43,711
That way, a surprise doesn't push you into debt.

43
00:01:43,731 --> 00:01:46,233
Second: pay yourself first, not last.

44
00:01:46,253 --> 00:01:49,193
As soon as your paycheck arrives, move a fixed amount, even a

45
00:01:49,213 --> 00:01:49,908
small one.

46
00:01:49,928 --> 00:01:52,807
Even better, make it automatic: set up a transfer for the day

47
00:01:52,827 --> 00:01:55,367
after payday, so you never even see that money.

48
00:01:55,387 --> 00:01:56,713
Start with ten percent.

49
00:01:56,733 --> 00:01:58,600
If that's too much, start with five.

50
00:01:58,620 --> 00:02:00,620
The habit matters more than the amount.

51
00:02:00,640 --> 00:02:04,054
And one more thing: if you have credit card debt, pay it off first.

52
00:02:04,074 --> 00:02:06,820
Credit card rates are often above twenty percent a year.

53
00:02:06,840 --> 00:02:09,190
That's compound interest working against you.

54
00:02:09,210 --> 00:02:11,160
Now, let's talk about safe investments.

55
00:02:11,180 --> 00:02:14,847
I'll be honest with you, as a friend: zero risk doesn't exist.

56
00:02:14,867 --> 00:02:15,753
There's a ladder.

57
00:02:15,773 --> 00:02:19,012
At the bottom, savings accounts and deposits: in many countries

58
00:02:19,032 --> 00:02:21,830
they're protected up to a limit, like two hundred fifty thousand

59
00:02:21,850 --> 00:02:25,230
dollars in the US, or one hundred thousand euros in Europe.

60
00:02:25,250 --> 00:02:26,420
But they pay little.

61
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:30,140
Then government bonds: low risk, but if you sell before they mature,

62
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:31,449
the price can change.

63
00:02:31,469 --> 00:02:34,480
Higher up, stocks and the funds that hold them: over the long

64
00:02:34,500 --> 00:02:37,380
run they've returned more, but they can also drop a lot.

65
00:02:37,400 --> 00:02:39,323
More return means more risk.

66
00:02:39,343 --> 00:02:40,190
Always.

67
00:02:41,280 --> 00:02:42,600
So how do you choose?

68
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:45,893
Ask yourself one question: when will I need this money?

69
00:02:45,913 --> 00:02:48,700
Money for the next few years stays at the bottom of the ladder.

70
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:51,860
Money you won't touch for ten years or more can climb higher,

71
00:02:51,880 --> 00:02:54,760
because time gives it the chance to recover from the drops.

72
00:02:54,780 --> 00:02:57,180
Now, three rules so you don't get burned.

73
00:02:57,200 --> 00:02:58,660
One: diversify.

74
00:02:58,680 --> 00:03:00,540
Don't put all your eggs in one basket.

75
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:03,864
Many people do it with index funds: one single fund that holds

76
00:03:03,884 --> 00:03:07,607
hundreds, even thousands of companies, often with very low fees.

77
00:03:07,627 --> 00:03:10,080
If one company fails, you barely notice.

78
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:11,740
Two: watch the fees.

79
00:03:11,760 --> 00:03:15,127
In Mark's example, if a product takes one percent a year, instead

80
00:03:15,147 --> 00:03:18,362
of sixty-nine thousand dollars you end up with about fifty-eight

81
00:03:18,382 --> 00:03:19,153
thousand.

82
00:03:19,173 --> 00:03:22,860
Three: be wary of anyone promising high, guaranteed returns.

83
00:03:22,880 --> 00:03:25,827
If it sounds too good to be true, it probably is.

84
00:03:25,847 --> 00:03:27,180
Often, it's a scam.

85
00:03:27,200 --> 00:03:28,500
So, let's recap.

86
00:03:28,520 --> 00:03:29,300
Start early.

87
00:03:29,320 --> 00:03:30,660
Build your emergency fund.

88
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:31,760
Pay yourself first.

89
00:03:31,780 --> 00:03:33,260
Get rid of expensive debt.

90
00:03:33,280 --> 00:03:36,684
Invest for the long term, diversified and with low fees.

91
00:03:36,704 --> 00:03:38,560
Time and patience will do the rest.

92
00:03:38,580 --> 00:03:42,060
And remember: this video explains the concepts, it's not financial

93
00:03:42,080 --> 00:03:42,860
advice.

94
00:03:42,880 --> 00:03:46,209
Before you invest, do your research, or talk to a professional.

95
00:03:46,229 --> 00:03:49,140
If this video helped you, hit like, subscribe to the channel,

96
00:03:49,160 --> 00:03:51,717
and tell me in the comments how old you were when you started

97
00:03:51,737 --> 00:03:52,360
saving.

98
00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:53,830
See you in the next lesson!
